 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed event revisions push to connected browsers over server-managed WebSocket sessions, backed by replayable revisions in the change store and reconnect catch-up in the browser. This accepts owning connection capacity and reconnect operations in exchange for fast updates without tying the board to a realtime provider. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected despite simpler stateless operation because of repeated read load across up to 800 simultaneous browsers and multi-second delivery lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected despite lower connection-management overhead because of recurring fees and provider coupling. See [`docs/decisions/change-delivery.md`](decisions/change-delivery.md).
